{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"ff8f1d6c-c539-4354-b998-9efecce18a15\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 1: The Community Garden\\n\\nResidents of a crowded neighbourhood turned an unused plot into a community garden. Families volunteered on weekends, while a nearby school supplied compost made from its kitchen waste. The garden produced vegetables, but its greatest value was social. People who had rarely spoken began exchanging seeds, recipes and advice. The organisers later added a shaded bench so that older residents could participate without doing heavy work.\\n\\nQuestion: What is the main idea of the passage?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 1: The Community Garden\\n\\nResidents of a crowded neighbourhood turned an unused plot into a community garden. Families volunteered on weekends, while a nearby school supplied compost made from its kitchen waste. The garden produced vegetables, but its greatest value was social. People who had rarely spoken began exchanging seeds, recipes and advice. The organisers later added a shaded bench so that older residents could participate without doing heavy work.\\n\\nQuestion: What is the main idea of the passage?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"A garden brought residents together while using vacant land productively.\",\"hi\":\"A garden brought residents together while using vacant land productively.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"The school replaced all classroom activities with gardening.\",\"hi\":\"The school replaced all classroom activities with gardening.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Older residents opposed the neighbourhood project.\",\"hi\":\"Older residents opposed the neighbourhood project.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"The garden was created mainly to sell vegetables.\",\"hi\":\"The garden was created mainly to sell vegetables.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"fef5ab8f-c9c5-45aa-9619-429c5a994111\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 1: Dashboards and Decisions\\n\\nPublic dashboards can make complex information easier to examine, but visibility does not automatically produce understanding. A chart may be accurate and still mislead if its scale hides variation or if a headline ignores uncertainty. Good dashboards therefore explain where data came from, when they were updated and what they cannot show. They also allow users to compare like with like. The aim is not to remove judgement from decision-making; it is to give judgement a clearer foundation.\\n\\nQuestion: Why should a dashboard state when data were updated?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 1: Dashboards and Decisions\\n\\nPublic dashboards can make complex information easier to examine, but visibility does not automatically produce understanding. A chart may be accurate and still mislead if its scale hides variation or if a headline ignores uncertainty. Good dashboards therefore explain where data came from, when they were updated and what they cannot show. They also allow users to compare like with like. The aim is not to remove judgement from decision-making; it is to give judgement a clearer foundation.\\n\\nQuestion: Why should a dashboard state when data were updated?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Older data are always legally invalid.\",\"hi\":\"Older data are always legally invalid.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Updates make every comparison unnecessary.\",\"hi\":\"Updates make every comparison unnecessary.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Users need to judge how current the information is.\",\"hi\":\"Users need to judge how current the information is.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"The date determines the colour of the chart.\",\"hi\":\"The date determines the colour of the chart.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Comprehension Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"f9a61d92-af4c-47b3-8fef-780b2a0869e4\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 2: The Note in the Atlas\\n\\nWhile arranging returned books, librarian Farid found a note inside an old atlas: “Look where the river meets the road.” He first assumed it was a clue to hidden money, but the map showed no such place. Then he noticed a child waiting near the desk. She explained that the note was part of a geography game she had designed for her younger brother. Farid helped her replace the note and suggested adding page numbers so that the next clue would be easier to follow.\\n\\nQuestion: What was the note’s actual purpose?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 2: The Note in the Atlas\\n\\nWhile arranging returned books, librarian Farid found a note inside an old atlas: “Look where the river meets the road.” He first assumed it was a clue to hidden money, but the map showed no such place. Then he noticed a child waiting near the desk. She explained that the note was part of a geography game she had designed for her younger brother. Farid helped her replace the note and suggested adding page numbers so that the next clue would be easier to follow.\\n\\nQuestion: What was the note’s actual purpose?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"It marked a construction site.\",\"hi\":\"It marked a construction site.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"It recorded a library fine.\",\"hi\":\"It recorded a library fine.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"It was part of a geography game.\",\"hi\":\"It was part of a geography game.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"It gave directions to Farid’s home.\",\"hi\":\"It gave directions to Farid’s home.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"f8aa5258-0c34-407c-a909-cc8cd4227229\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 1: Urban Heat Planning\\n\\nA city review of summer preparedness found that heat risk was not evenly distributed. Neighbourhoods with little shade and large paved areas stayed warmer for longer, while outdoor workers faced repeated exposure during peak afternoon hours. The review recommended cool-roof programmes, shaded bus stops, drinking-water points and work schedules that avoid the hottest period. It also warned that planting trees alone would not provide immediate relief because young trees take time to create shade. The report therefore favoured a mix of short-term protection and long-term design.\\n\\nQuestion: Which is a short-term protective measure mentioned?\",\"hi\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 1: Urban Heat Planning\\n\\nA city review of summer preparedness found that heat risk was not evenly distributed. Neighbourhoods with little shade and large paved areas stayed warmer for longer, while outdoor workers faced repeated exposure during peak afternoon hours. The review recommended cool-roof programmes, shaded bus stops, drinking-water points and work schedules that avoid the hottest period. It also warned that planting trees alone would not provide immediate relief because young trees take time to create shade. The report therefore favoured a mix of short-term protection and long-term design.\\n\\nQuestion: Which is a short-term protective measure mentioned?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Providing shaded bus stops\",\"hi\":\"Providing shaded bus stops\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Waiting for young trees to mature\",\"hi\":\"Waiting for young trees to mature\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Replacing all roads with forests immediately\",\"hi\":\"Replacing all roads with forests immediately\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Ending every form of outdoor work permanently\",\"hi\":\"Ending every form of outdoor work permanently\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"f7058eae-1b9e-43fb-aa7c-1ef33f834aa5\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 4: Quiet Hour\\n\\nA college introduced a daily quiet hour in one study hall. During that period, phone calls and group discussions were moved to other rooms. The rule was not intended to make the entire library silent; it simply created a predictable time and place for concentrated work. A student survey later showed that many learners used the hour to read difficult material or revise before tests.\\n\\nQuestion: Which word best describes the tone of the passage?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 4: Quiet Hour\\n\\nA college introduced a daily quiet hour in one study hall. During that period, phone calls and group discussions were moved to other rooms. The rule was not intended to make the entire library silent; it simply created a predictable time and place for concentrated work. A student survey later showed that many learners used the hour to read difficult material or revise before tests.\\n\\nQuestion: Which word best describes the tone of the passage?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Angry\",\"hi\":\"Angry\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Mocking\",\"hi\":\"Mocking\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Informative\",\"hi\":\"Informative\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Fearful\",\"hi\":\"Fearful\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"f195e72d-7607-4b44-9c1a-681fa86e0421\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 3: The Ferry Painter\\n\\nEvery morning, Dev crossed the river by ferry and sketched the changing light on the water. One foggy day, the ferry was delayed, and impatient passengers complained. Dev used the extra time to draw their blurred reflections in the cabin windows. Weeks later, he displayed the sketch at a local exhibition. Several passengers recognised themselves and laughed at how serious they had looked. What had felt like wasted time became the subject of his most popular picture.\\n\\nQuestion: What transformation is central to the story?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 3: The Ferry Painter\\n\\nEvery morning, Dev crossed the river by ferry and sketched the changing light on the water. One foggy day, the ferry was delayed, and impatient passengers complained. Dev used the extra time to draw their blurred reflections in the cabin windows. Weeks later, he displayed the sketch at a local exhibition. Several passengers recognised themselves and laughed at how serious they had looked. What had felt like wasted time became the subject of his most popular picture.\\n\\nQuestion: What transformation is central to the story?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"A ferry became an exhibition hall.\",\"hi\":\"A ferry became an exhibition hall.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"An irritating delay became a creative opportunity.\",\"hi\":\"An irritating delay became a creative opportunity.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"A sketch caused the fog.\",\"hi\":\"A sketch caused the fog.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Passengers learned to operate the ferry.\",\"hi\":\"Passengers learned to operate the ferry.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"ef509e23-1bc5-486a-8e83-e44a3491a9dc\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 4\\n\\nReliable public transport gives commuters an alternative to private vehicles. Frequency matters because passengers are less likely to use a service that (1) ___ long waits. Accurate information is equally valuable: when delays occur, travellers can (2) ___ their plans. Clean stations and safe walking routes also shape the overall (3) ___. Investment should therefore consider the complete journey, not (4) ___ the time spent inside a bus or train. A network becomes attractive when its parts work (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (2).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 4\\n\\nReliable public transport gives commuters an alternative to private vehicles. Frequency matters because passengers are less likely to use a service that (1) ___ long waits. Accurate information is equally valuable: when delays occur, travellers can (2) ___ their plans. Clean stations and safe walking routes also shape the overall (3) ___. Investment should therefore consider the complete journey, not (4) ___ the time spent inside a bus or train. A network becomes attractive when its parts work (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (2).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"ignore\",\"hi\":\"ignore\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"adjust\",\"hi\":\"adjust\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"abolish\",\"hi\":\"abolish\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"conceal\",\"hi\":\"conceal\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"eb6c1cba-c684-4de5-8072-ac8703a248bd\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 2\\n\\nCities can reduce water waste by paying attention to small leaks. A dripping tap may appear (1) ___, yet many such leaks together can waste a large quantity of water. Regular inspection allows faults to be repaired (2) ___ they become expensive. Clear bills can also help households (3) ___ unusual changes in consumption. However, information alone is not (4) ___; people need simple ways to report problems. Conservation works best when authorities and residents act (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (5).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 2\\n\\nCities can reduce water waste by paying attention to small leaks. A dripping tap may appear (1) ___, yet many such leaks together can waste a large quantity of water. Regular inspection allows faults to be repaired (2) ___ they become expensive. Clear bills can also help households (3) ___ unusual changes in consumption. However, information alone is not (4) ___; people need simple ways to report problems. Conservation works best when authorities and residents act (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (5).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"separately\",\"hi\":\"separately\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"reluctantly\",\"hi\":\"reluctantly\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"together\",\"hi\":\"together\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"occasionally\",\"hi\":\"occasionally\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"ea750721-b2d2-40b0-b011-ddad448e4f1c\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 3: The Repair Desk\\n\\nOnce a month, volunteers at a town hall helped people repair small household items. Visitors brought lamps, clocks, toys and fans that might otherwise have been discarded. The volunteers did not promise to fix everything; instead, they explained what was wrong and showed owners how repairs were attempted. Even unsuccessful cases were useful because people learned which parts could be reused safely.\\n\\nQuestion: What was the primary purpose of the repair desk?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 3: The Repair Desk\\n\\nOnce a month, volunteers at a town hall helped people repair small household items. Visitors brought lamps, clocks, toys and fans that might otherwise have been discarded. The volunteers did not promise to fix everything; instead, they explained what was wrong and showed owners how repairs were attempted. Even unsuccessful cases were useful because people learned which parts could be reused safely.\\n\\nQuestion: What was the primary purpose of the repair desk?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"To collect fees for replacing all appliances\",\"hi\":\"To collect fees for replacing all appliances\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"To sell new household products\",\"hi\":\"To sell new household products\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"To help people repair items and learn from the process\",\"hi\":\"To help people repair items and learn from the process\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"To prevent owners from observing repairs\",\"hi\":\"To prevent owners from observing repairs\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"e68b0ddf-f87b-4f2e-a61e-3b9528f4c042\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 3\\n\\nOnline training is useful only when participants can apply what they learn. Short demonstrations should be followed by tasks that (1) ___ the same skill. Immediate practice strengthens memory and reveals (2) ___ the instructions were clear. Trainers can then answer questions before confusion becomes (3) ___. A well-designed course also gives learners some (4) ___ over pace, while maintaining firm deadlines. This combination keeps the programme flexible (5) ___ focused.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (1).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 3\\n\\nOnline training is useful only when participants can apply what they learn. Short demonstrations should be followed by tasks that (1) ___ the same skill. Immediate practice strengthens memory and reveals (2) ___ the instructions were clear. Trainers can then answer questions before confusion becomes (3) ___. A well-designed course also gives learners some (4) ___ over pace, while maintaining firm deadlines. This combination keeps the programme flexible (5) ___ focused.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (1).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"escape\",\"hi\":\"escape\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"cancel\",\"hi\":\"cancel\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"require\",\"hi\":\"require\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"conceal\",\"hi\":\"conceal\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"e55f9b68-bdc3-4b52-88dd-3548e26531e2\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 2: A Library on Wheels\\n\\nA district library converted an old bus into a mobile reading room. Every Tuesday, the bus visited three villages that had no permanent library. Its shelves carried children’s stories, exam guides and newspapers. Borrowers could return books during the next visit. After six months, the library noticed that teenagers were requesting more science books, so it changed part of the collection to match their interests.\\n\\nQuestion: When could borrowers return books?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 2: A Library on Wheels\\n\\nA district library converted an old bus into a mobile reading room. Every Tuesday, the bus visited three villages that had no permanent library. Its shelves carried children’s stories, exam guides and newspapers. Borrowers could return books during the next visit. After six months, the library noticed that teenagers were requesting more science books, so it changed part of the collection to match their interests.\\n\\nQuestion: When could borrowers return books?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Only at the district headquarters\",\"hi\":\"Only at the district headquarters\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"During the next visit\",\"hi\":\"During the next visit\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"On the same day\",\"hi\":\"On the same day\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"After one full year\",\"hi\":\"After one full year\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"dd0de231-99c8-4af2-b7ec-8ddcce466f37\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: How does the author treat safety concerns?\",\"hi\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: How does the author treat safety concerns?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"They are imaginary and can be ignored.\",\"hi\":\"They are imaginary and can be ignored.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"They justify blocking every repair.\",\"hi\":\"They justify blocking every repair.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"They are genuine but should be handled through proportionate rules.\",\"hi\":\"They are genuine but should be handled through proportionate rules.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"They matter only for old devices.\",\"hi\":\"They matter only for old devices.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"db12f0b7-68bd-4990-8bf3-d6d1d0057146\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 2: A Library on Wheels\\n\\nA district library converted an old bus into a mobile reading room. Every Tuesday, the bus visited three villages that had no permanent library. Its shelves carried children’s stories, exam guides and newspapers. Borrowers could return books during the next visit. After six months, the library noticed that teenagers were requesting more science books, so it changed part of the collection to match their interests.\\n\\nQuestion: Why was the collection changed after six months?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 2: A Library on Wheels\\n\\nA district library converted an old bus into a mobile reading room. Every Tuesday, the bus visited three villages that had no permanent library. Its shelves carried children’s stories, exam guides and newspapers. Borrowers could return books during the next visit. After six months, the library noticed that teenagers were requesting more science books, so it changed part of the collection to match their interests.\\n\\nQuestion: Why was the collection changed after six months?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Children stopped reading stories entirely.\",\"hi\":\"Children stopped reading stories entirely.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Teenagers showed greater interest in science books.\",\"hi\":\"Teenagers showed greater interest in science books.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"The bus could no longer carry newspapers.\",\"hi\":\"The bus could no longer carry newspapers.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"The villages opened permanent libraries.\",\"hi\":\"The villages opened permanent libraries.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"d9fbfc58-c899-4ece-bf2c-2026854b3d24\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 2: The Value of Urban Wetlands\\n\\nAn urban wetland may look like unused land, especially during a dry season. Yet it can store rainwater, support birds and reduce pressure on drainage systems. Building over such an area may create immediate economic value, but it can also transfer hidden costs to nearby neighbourhoods through greater flood risk. Protecting a wetland does not mean preventing all public access. Paths and viewing areas can be designed carefully, provided that the most sensitive zones remain undisturbed.\\n\\nQuestion: Why might an urban wetland be mistaken for unused land?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 2: The Value of Urban Wetlands\\n\\nAn urban wetland may look like unused land, especially during a dry season. Yet it can store rainwater, support birds and reduce pressure on drainage systems. Building over such an area may create immediate economic value, but it can also transfer hidden costs to nearby neighbourhoods through greater flood risk. Protecting a wetland does not mean preventing all public access. Paths and viewing areas can be designed carefully, provided that the most sensitive zones remain undisturbed.\\n\\nQuestion: Why might an urban wetland be mistaken for unused land?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"It contains no plants during any season.\",\"hi\":\"It contains no plants during any season.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"It is always closed to the public.\",\"hi\":\"It is always closed to the public.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Its ecological functions may not be obvious, especially when it is dry.\",\"hi\":\"Its ecological functions may not be obvious, especially when it is dry.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"It has already been prepared for construction.\",\"hi\":\"It has already been prepared for construction.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Comprehension Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"d93b41d8-6dce-4f4b-896d-747507e8955c\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 1\\n\\nProgress in any skill rarely comes from one dramatic effort. It usually depends on (1) ___ practice, honest feedback and enough patience to continue after mistakes. A learner who records errors can (2) ___ patterns that would otherwise remain hidden. This makes each new attempt more (3) ___. Rest is equally important because attention declines when the mind is (4) ___. A balanced routine is therefore more sustainable (5) ___ a schedule based only on pressure.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (4).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 1\\n\\nProgress in any skill rarely comes from one dramatic effort. It usually depends on (1) ___ practice, honest feedback and enough patience to continue after mistakes. A learner who records errors can (2) ___ patterns that would otherwise remain hidden. This makes each new attempt more (3) ___. Rest is equally important because attention declines when the mind is (4) ___. A balanced routine is therefore more sustainable (5) ___ a schedule based only on pressure.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (4).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"refreshed\",\"hi\":\"refreshed\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"rested\",\"hi\":\"rested\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"exhausted\",\"hi\":\"exhausted\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"alert\",\"hi\":\"alert\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"d6e2e0a3-f0c4-4335-9a15-7f3cc55deff7\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 4: Communicating Scientific Uncertainty\\n\\nUncertainty in a scientific estimate is not evidence that the estimate is useless. It describes the range within which available evidence supports a conclusion. Problems arise when uncertainty is either hidden or exaggerated. Hiding it creates false confidence; exaggerating it can make well-supported findings appear arbitrary. Clear communication states what is known, what remains uncertain and whether new evidence is likely to change the conclusion substantially.\\n\\nQuestion: Which statement best reflects the passage?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 4: Communicating Scientific Uncertainty\\n\\nUncertainty in a scientific estimate is not evidence that the estimate is useless. It describes the range within which available evidence supports a conclusion. Problems arise when uncertainty is either hidden or exaggerated. Hiding it creates false confidence; exaggerating it can make well-supported findings appear arbitrary. Clear communication states what is known, what remains uncertain and whether new evidence is likely to change the conclusion substantially.\\n\\nQuestion: Which statement best reflects the passage?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Any uncertainty makes evidence worthless.\",\"hi\":\"Any uncertainty makes evidence worthless.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Acknowledged uncertainty can strengthen rather than weaken honest communication.\",\"hi\":\"Acknowledged uncertainty can strengthen rather than weaken honest communication.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Scientific estimates should never be revised.\",\"hi\":\"Scientific estimates should never be revised.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Confidence is preferable to accuracy.\",\"hi\":\"Confidence is preferable to accuracy.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Comprehension Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"d30762b5-d10b-4741-88f5-60c12f0c8a64\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 2\\n\\nCities can reduce water waste by paying attention to small leaks. A dripping tap may appear (1) ___, yet many such leaks together can waste a large quantity of water. Regular inspection allows faults to be repaired (2) ___ they become expensive. Clear bills can also help households (3) ___ unusual changes in consumption. However, information alone is not (4) ___; people need simple ways to report problems. Conservation works best when authorities and residents act (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (2).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 2\\n\\nCities can reduce water waste by paying attention to small leaks. A dripping tap may appear (1) ___, yet many such leaks together can waste a large quantity of water. Regular inspection allows faults to be repaired (2) ___ they become expensive. Clear bills can also help households (3) ___ unusual changes in consumption. However, information alone is not (4) ___; people need simple ways to report problems. Conservation works best when authorities and residents act (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (2).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"unless\",\"hi\":\"unless\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"although\",\"hi\":\"although\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"whereas\",\"hi\":\"whereas\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"before\",\"hi\":\"before\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"d1d28c28-9c6f-439f-9fbb-b4844389e81e\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 3: Inclusive Digital Services\\n\\nAn assessment of online public services found that digitisation reduced travel for many users but created new barriers for others. Some applicants lacked reliable internet access; others could open a form but struggled with unclear instructions or inaccessible verification steps. The assessment recommended simpler language, low-bandwidth pages, assisted service counters and a clear way to save partially completed forms. It measured success not by the number of services placed online, but by the share of users able to complete them without avoidable failure.\\n\\nQuestion: Why were assisted service counters recommended?\",\"hi\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 3: Inclusive Digital Services\\n\\nAn assessment of online public services found that digitisation reduced travel for many users but created new barriers for others. Some applicants lacked reliable internet access; others could open a form but struggled with unclear instructions or inaccessible verification steps. The assessment recommended simpler language, low-bandwidth pages, assisted service counters and a clear way to save partially completed forms. It measured success not by the number of services placed online, but by the share of users able to complete them without avoidable failure.\\n\\nQuestion: Why were assisted service counters recommended?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"To replace every online service\",\"hi\":\"To replace every online service\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"To increase mandatory travel for all users\",\"hi\":\"To increase mandatory travel for all users\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"To make pages use more bandwidth\",\"hi\":\"To make pages use more bandwidth\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"To support users who could not complete the process independently\",\"hi\":\"To support users who could not complete the process independently\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"cfda813e-2a90-413c-859b-92c249d48805\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 2: The Note in the Atlas\\n\\nWhile arranging returned books, librarian Farid found a note inside an old atlas: “Look where the river meets the road.” He first assumed it was a clue to hidden money, but the map showed no such place. Then he noticed a child waiting near the desk. She explained that the note was part of a geography game she had designed for her younger brother. Farid helped her replace the note and suggested adding page numbers so that the next clue would be easier to follow.\\n\\nQuestion: Where did Farid find the note?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 2: The Note in the Atlas\\n\\nWhile arranging returned books, librarian Farid found a note inside an old atlas: “Look where the river meets the road.” He first assumed it was a clue to hidden money, but the map showed no such place. Then he noticed a child waiting near the desk. She explained that the note was part of a geography game she had designed for her younger brother. Farid helped her replace the note and suggested adding page numbers so that the next clue would be easier to follow.\\n\\nQuestion: Where did Farid find the note?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Under the library desk\",\"hi\":\"Under the library desk\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Beside a real river\",\"hi\":\"Beside a real river\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Inside an old atlas\",\"hi\":\"Inside an old atlas\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"In a money box\",\"hi\":\"In a money box\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"ce98b50c-137f-4209-9f47-9ed138fe3424\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 3: Inclusive Digital Services\\n\\nAn assessment of online public services found that digitisation reduced travel for many users but created new barriers for others. Some applicants lacked reliable internet access; others could open a form but struggled with unclear instructions or inaccessible verification steps. The assessment recommended simpler language, low-bandwidth pages, assisted service counters and a clear way to save partially completed forms. It measured success not by the number of services placed online, but by the share of users able to complete them without avoidable failure.\\n\\nQuestion: What broader principle does the passage support?\",\"hi\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 3: Inclusive Digital Services\\n\\nAn assessment of online public services found that digitisation reduced travel for many users but created new barriers for others. Some applicants lacked reliable internet access; others could open a form but struggled with unclear instructions or inaccessible verification steps. The assessment recommended simpler language, low-bandwidth pages, assisted service counters and a clear way to save partially completed forms. It measured success not by the number of services placed online, but by the share of users able to complete them without avoidable failure.\\n\\nQuestion: What broader principle does the passage support?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"A service is inclusive once a website exists.\",\"hi\":\"A service is inclusive once a website exists.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Complex language improves public trust.\",\"hi\":\"Complex language improves public trust.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Access should be judged by practical usability, not availability alone.\",\"hi\":\"Access should be judged by practical usability, not availability alone.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Offline assistance prevents digital progress.\",\"hi\":\"Offline assistance prevents digital progress.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"cda6ac55-935f-4c9f-9939-d375f25c181f\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 4: Quiet Hour\\n\\nA college introduced a daily quiet hour in one study hall. During that period, phone calls and group discussions were moved to other rooms. The rule was not intended to make the entire library silent; it simply created a predictable time and place for concentrated work. A student survey later showed that many learners used the hour to read difficult material or revise before tests.\\n\\nQuestion: What did the college introduce?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 4: Quiet Hour\\n\\nA college introduced a daily quiet hour in one study hall. During that period, phone calls and group discussions were moved to other rooms. The rule was not intended to make the entire library silent; it simply created a predictable time and place for concentrated work. A student survey later showed that many learners used the hour to read difficult material or revise before tests.\\n\\nQuestion: What did the college introduce?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"A daily quiet hour in one study hall\",\"hi\":\"A daily quiet hour in one study hall\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"A ban on all phones across the campus\",\"hi\":\"A ban on all phones across the campus\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"A weekly sports period\",\"hi\":\"A weekly sports period\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"A new examination system\",\"hi\":\"A new examination system\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"cbb9b8fd-d021-4e12-b1e5-b74ad3a8be88\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: Which statement best captures the author’s argument?\",\"hi\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: Which statement best captures the author’s argument?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Repair access can reduce waste without ignoring legitimate safeguards.\",\"hi\":\"Repair access can reduce waste without ignoring legitimate safeguards.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Environmental goals require the removal of all safety rules.\",\"hi\":\"Environmental goals require the removal of all safety rules.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Intellectual property is unrelated to repair policy.\",\"hi\":\"Intellectual property is unrelated to repair policy.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Convenience for manufacturers should be the only standard.\",\"hi\":\"Convenience for manufacturers should be the only standard.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"ca9fd804-2f16-421d-801a-105d35d965a0\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 4: Quiet Hour\\n\\nA college introduced a daily quiet hour in one study hall. During that period, phone calls and group discussions were moved to other rooms. The rule was not intended to make the entire library silent; it simply created a predictable time and place for concentrated work. A student survey later showed that many learners used the hour to read difficult material or revise before tests.\\n\\nQuestion: What was the purpose of the quiet hour?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 4: Quiet Hour\\n\\nA college introduced a daily quiet hour in one study hall. During that period, phone calls and group discussions were moved to other rooms. The rule was not intended to make the entire library silent; it simply created a predictable time and place for concentrated work. A student survey later showed that many learners used the hour to read difficult material or revise before tests.\\n\\nQuestion: What was the purpose of the quiet hour?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"To make the entire library permanently silent\",\"hi\":\"To make the entire library permanently silent\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"To provide a predictable setting for concentrated work\",\"hi\":\"To provide a predictable setting for concentrated work\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"To reduce the number of students\",\"hi\":\"To reduce the number of students\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"To replace classroom teaching\",\"hi\":\"To replace classroom teaching\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"c68127c2-92b4-4c1f-9c6f-5c02f3362aad\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 3: The Ferry Painter\\n\\nEvery morning, Dev crossed the river by ferry and sketched the changing light on the water. One foggy day, the ferry was delayed, and impatient passengers complained. Dev used the extra time to draw their blurred reflections in the cabin windows. Weeks later, he displayed the sketch at a local exhibition. Several passengers recognised themselves and laughed at how serious they had looked. What had felt like wasted time became the subject of his most popular picture.\\n\\nQuestion: Why were the passengers impatient?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 3: The Ferry Painter\\n\\nEvery morning, Dev crossed the river by ferry and sketched the changing light on the water. One foggy day, the ferry was delayed, and impatient passengers complained. Dev used the extra time to draw their blurred reflections in the cabin windows. Weeks later, he displayed the sketch at a local exhibition. Several passengers recognised themselves and laughed at how serious they had looked. What had felt like wasted time became the subject of his most popular picture.\\n\\nQuestion: Why were the passengers impatient?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Dev refused to show his sketch.\",\"hi\":\"Dev refused to show his sketch.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"The exhibition opened late.\",\"hi\":\"The exhibition opened late.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"The ferry was delayed by fog.\",\"hi\":\"The ferry was delayed by fog.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"The river had dried up.\",\"hi\":\"The river had dried up.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"c3670324-9a56-4d00-bee5-08c996bcf5ba\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 1: Dashboards and Decisions\\n\\nPublic dashboards can make complex information easier to examine, but visibility does not automatically produce understanding. A chart may be accurate and still mislead if its scale hides variation or if a headline ignores uncertainty. Good dashboards therefore explain where data came from, when they were updated and what they cannot show. They also allow users to compare like with like. The aim is not to remove judgement from decision-making; it is to give judgement a clearer foundation.\\n\\nQuestion: According to the author, data should:\",\"hi\":\"Passage 1: Dashboards and Decisions\\n\\nPublic dashboards can make complex information easier to examine, but visibility does not automatically produce understanding. A chart may be accurate and still mislead if its scale hides variation or if a headline ignores uncertainty. Good dashboards therefore explain where data came from, when they were updated and what they cannot show. They also allow users to compare like with like. The aim is not to remove judgement from decision-making; it is to give judgement a clearer foundation.\\n\\nQuestion: According to the author, data should:\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"eliminate human judgement completely\",\"hi\":\"eliminate human judgement completely\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"support informed judgement rather than replace it\",\"hi\":\"support informed judgement rather than replace it\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"be hidden until uncertainty disappears\",\"hi\":\"be hidden until uncertainty disappears\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"be reduced to a single headline\",\"hi\":\"be reduced to a single headline\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Comprehension Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"bf2acbd7-6609-4748-8d05-31da681300ea\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 3: Decision Fatigue\\n\\nPeople often assume that more choice always improves a decision. In practice, a long series of minor choices can consume attention needed for an important one. This does not mean that choice is undesirable. It suggests that routine decisions should be simplified where possible. A prepared checklist, a fixed time for recurring tasks or a clear default can reduce mental effort without removing meaningful control. The best simplification preserves the decisions that genuinely require thought.\\n\\nQuestion: Why can many minor choices be harmful?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 3: Decision Fatigue\\n\\nPeople often assume that more choice always improves a decision. In practice, a long series of minor choices can consume attention needed for an important one. This does not mean that choice is undesirable. It suggests that routine decisions should be simplified where possible. A prepared checklist, a fixed time for recurring tasks or a clear default can reduce mental effort without removing meaningful control. The best simplification preserves the decisions that genuinely require thought.\\n\\nQuestion: Why can many minor choices be harmful?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"They can use attention needed for more important decisions.\",\"hi\":\"They can use attention needed for more important decisions.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"They permanently remove all personal control.\",\"hi\":\"They permanently remove all personal control.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"They make checklists impossible to use.\",\"hi\":\"They make checklists impossible to use.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"They ensure that every choice is meaningful.\",\"hi\":\"They ensure that every choice is meaningful.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Comprehension Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"bdcf0bf3-a6f5-4961-b326-9000cb2c83dc\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: What problem does the editorial identify?\",\"hi\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: What problem does the editorial identify?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Whole devices are often replaced because a small part fails.\",\"hi\":\"Whole devices are often replaced because a small part fails.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Consumers are legally forced to repair forever.\",\"hi\":\"Consumers are legally forced to repair forever.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Spare parts always create safety risks.\",\"hi\":\"Spare parts always create safety risks.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Manufacturers provide too much diagnostic information.\",\"hi\":\"Manufacturers provide too much diagnostic information.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"bb5627a4-b690-4321-b953-488d1deecff7\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 3: The Repair Desk\\n\\nOnce a month, volunteers at a town hall helped people repair small household items. Visitors brought lamps, clocks, toys and fans that might otherwise have been discarded. The volunteers did not promise to fix everything; instead, they explained what was wrong and showed owners how repairs were attempted. Even unsuccessful cases were useful because people learned which parts could be reused safely.\\n\\nQuestion: The phrase “might otherwise have been discarded” means the items:\",\"hi\":\"Passage 3: The Repair Desk\\n\\nOnce a month, volunteers at a town hall helped people repair small household items. Visitors brought lamps, clocks, toys and fans that might otherwise have been discarded. The volunteers did not promise to fix everything; instead, they explained what was wrong and showed owners how repairs were attempted. Even unsuccessful cases were useful because people learned which parts could be reused safely.\\n\\nQuestion: The phrase “might otherwise have been discarded” means the items:\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"were required to be sold immediately\",\"hi\":\"were required to be sold immediately\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"had already been repaired elsewhere\",\"hi\":\"had already been repaired elsewhere\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"could have been thrown away if no repair was attempted\",\"hi\":\"could have been thrown away if no repair was attempted\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"were too valuable to be used\",\"hi\":\"were too valuable to be used\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"b76a5c19-a4d7-4617-bfce-e90b7412423e\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 4\\n\\nReliable public transport gives commuters an alternative to private vehicles. Frequency matters because passengers are less likely to use a service that (1) ___ long waits. Accurate information is equally valuable: when delays occur, travellers can (2) ___ their plans. Clean stations and safe walking routes also shape the overall (3) ___. Investment should therefore consider the complete journey, not (4) ___ the time spent inside a bus or train. A network becomes attractive when its parts work (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (1).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 4\\n\\nReliable public transport gives commuters an alternative to private vehicles. Frequency matters because passengers are less likely to use a service that (1) ___ long waits. Accurate information is equally valuable: when delays occur, travellers can (2) ___ their plans. Clean stations and safe walking routes also shape the overall (3) ___. Investment should therefore consider the complete journey, not (4) ___ the time spent inside a bus or train. A network becomes attractive when its parts work (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (1).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"involves\",\"hi\":\"involves\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"prevents\",\"hi\":\"prevents\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"removes\",\"hi\":\"removes\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"shortens\",\"hi\":\"shortens\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"b346373f-915c-4b6d-908e-1bbabc0bcc62\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 2: The Note in the Atlas\\n\\nWhile arranging returned books, librarian Farid found a note inside an old atlas: “Look where the river meets the road.” He first assumed it was a clue to hidden money, but the map showed no such place. Then he noticed a child waiting near the desk. She explained that the note was part of a geography game she had designed for her younger brother. Farid helped her replace the note and suggested adding page numbers so that the next clue would be easier to follow.\\n\\nQuestion: What did Farid initially imagine?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 2: The Note in the Atlas\\n\\nWhile arranging returned books, librarian Farid found a note inside an old atlas: “Look where the river meets the road.” He first assumed it was a clue to hidden money, but the map showed no such place. Then he noticed a child waiting near the desk. She explained that the note was part of a geography game she had designed for her younger brother. Farid helped her replace the note and suggested adding page numbers so that the next clue would be easier to follow.\\n\\nQuestion: What did Farid initially imagine?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"The note might lead to hidden money.\",\"hi\":\"The note might lead to hidden money.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"The atlas belonged to him.\",\"hi\":\"The atlas belonged to him.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"The child had damaged the book.\",\"hi\":\"The child had damaged the book.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"The road on the map was closed.\",\"hi\":\"The road on the map was closed.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"b31ac415-d38b-4844-8cf9-b83d4754a458\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 1\\n\\nProgress in any skill rarely comes from one dramatic effort. It usually depends on (1) ___ practice, honest feedback and enough patience to continue after mistakes. A learner who records errors can (2) ___ patterns that would otherwise remain hidden. This makes each new attempt more (3) ___. Rest is equally important because attention declines when the mind is (4) ___. A balanced routine is therefore more sustainable (5) ___ a schedule based only on pressure.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (5).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 1\\n\\nProgress in any skill rarely comes from one dramatic effort. It usually depends on (1) ___ practice, honest feedback and enough patience to continue after mistakes. A learner who records errors can (2) ___ patterns that would otherwise remain hidden. This makes each new attempt more (3) ___. Rest is equally important because attention declines when the mind is (4) ___. A balanced routine is therefore more sustainable (5) ___ a schedule based only on pressure.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (5).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"during\",\"hi\":\"during\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"without\",\"hi\":\"without\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"than\",\"hi\":\"than\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"beyond\",\"hi\":\"beyond\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"b2844729-2d31-4c94-9889-f016d832709a\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 4: Communicating Scientific Uncertainty\\n\\nUncertainty in a scientific estimate is not evidence that the estimate is useless. It describes the range within which available evidence supports a conclusion. Problems arise when uncertainty is either hidden or exaggerated. Hiding it creates false confidence; exaggerating it can make well-supported findings appear arbitrary. Clear communication states what is known, what remains uncertain and whether new evidence is likely to change the conclusion substantially.\\n\\nQuestion: How can exaggerating uncertainty distort understanding?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 4: Communicating Scientific Uncertainty\\n\\nUncertainty in a scientific estimate is not evidence that the estimate is useless. It describes the range within which available evidence supports a conclusion. Problems arise when uncertainty is either hidden or exaggerated. Hiding it creates false confidence; exaggerating it can make well-supported findings appear arbitrary. Clear communication states what is known, what remains uncertain and whether new evidence is likely to change the conclusion substantially.\\n\\nQuestion: How can exaggerating uncertainty distort understanding?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"It can make strong findings seem arbitrary.\",\"hi\":\"It can make strong findings seem arbitrary.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"It removes the need for communication.\",\"hi\":\"It removes the need for communication.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"It turns estimates into exact facts.\",\"hi\":\"It turns estimates into exact facts.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"It proves that evidence is false.\",\"hi\":\"It proves that evidence is false.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Comprehension Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"ae4ac336-de79-47a9-8f62-6829a694e701\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 1: The Community Garden\\n\\nResidents of a crowded neighbourhood turned an unused plot into a community garden. Families volunteered on weekends, while a nearby school supplied compost made from its kitchen waste. The garden produced vegetables, but its greatest value was social. People who had rarely spoken began exchanging seeds, recipes and advice. The organisers later added a shaded bench so that older residents could participate without doing heavy work.\\n\\nQuestion: Who supplied compost to the garden?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 1: The Community Garden\\n\\nResidents of a crowded neighbourhood turned an unused plot into a community garden. Families volunteered on weekends, while a nearby school supplied compost made from its kitchen waste. The garden produced vegetables, but its greatest value was social. People who had rarely spoken began exchanging seeds, recipes and advice. The organisers later added a shaded bench so that older residents could participate without doing heavy work.\\n\\nQuestion: Who supplied compost to the garden?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"A nearby school\",\"hi\":\"A nearby school\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"The municipal market\",\"hi\":\"The municipal market\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"A private nursery\",\"hi\":\"A private nursery\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"The older residents alone\",\"hi\":\"The older residents alone\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"adeab29d-56df-4df8-b85c-71d1b95b5045\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 4: Quiet Hour\\n\\nA college introduced a daily quiet hour in one study hall. During that period, phone calls and group discussions were moved to other rooms. The rule was not intended to make the entire library silent; it simply created a predictable time and place for concentrated work. A student survey later showed that many learners used the hour to read difficult material or revise before tests.\\n\\nQuestion: Where were phone calls and group discussions allowed during the quiet hour?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 4: Quiet Hour\\n\\nA college introduced a daily quiet hour in one study hall. During that period, phone calls and group discussions were moved to other rooms. The rule was not intended to make the entire library silent; it simply created a predictable time and place for concentrated work. A student survey later showed that many learners used the hour to read difficult material or revise before tests.\\n\\nQuestion: Where were phone calls and group discussions allowed during the quiet hour?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Nowhere on campus\",\"hi\":\"Nowhere on campus\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Inside the same study hall\",\"hi\":\"Inside the same study hall\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Only outside the college gate\",\"hi\":\"Only outside the college gate\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"In other rooms\",\"hi\":\"In other rooms\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"adbd9200-0457-4aa6-9f16-91902a55b3dc\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 2: The Note in the Atlas\\n\\nWhile arranging returned books, librarian Farid found a note inside an old atlas: “Look where the river meets the road.” He first assumed it was a clue to hidden money, but the map showed no such place. Then he noticed a child waiting near the desk. She explained that the note was part of a geography game she had designed for her younger brother. Farid helped her replace the note and suggested adding page numbers so that the next clue would be easier to follow.\\n\\nQuestion: Which trait does Farid display at the end?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 2: The Note in the Atlas\\n\\nWhile arranging returned books, librarian Farid found a note inside an old atlas: “Look where the river meets the road.” He first assumed it was a clue to hidden money, but the map showed no such place. Then he noticed a child waiting near the desk. She explained that the note was part of a geography game she had designed for her younger brother. Farid helped her replace the note and suggested adding page numbers so that the next clue would be easier to follow.\\n\\nQuestion: Which trait does Farid display at the end?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Jealousy\",\"hi\":\"Jealousy\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Carelessness\",\"hi\":\"Carelessness\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Suspicion only\",\"hi\":\"Suspicion only\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Helpfulness\",\"hi\":\"Helpfulness\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"a89b8167-fbf3-4abc-a7ef-396660a3c83b\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 4: The Envelope of Seeds\\n\\nNora received an unlabelled envelope of seeds from her aunt. Instead of planting all of them in one place, she divided them among four pots and changed only one condition in each: sunlight, water, soil or temperature. She kept notes for three weeks. Two pots produced similar shoots, one grew slowly and one did not sprout. Nora could not identify the plant yet, but she had learned which conditions helped the seeds begin to grow.\\n\\nQuestion: Why did Nora use four pots?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 4: The Envelope of Seeds\\n\\nNora received an unlabelled envelope of seeds from her aunt. Instead of planting all of them in one place, she divided them among four pots and changed only one condition in each: sunlight, water, soil or temperature. She kept notes for three weeks. Two pots produced similar shoots, one grew slowly and one did not sprout. Nora could not identify the plant yet, but she had learned which conditions helped the seeds begin to grow.\\n\\nQuestion: Why did Nora use four pots?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"To compare how different conditions affected the seeds\",\"hi\":\"To compare how different conditions affected the seeds\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"To decorate four separate rooms\",\"hi\":\"To decorate four separate rooms\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"To give every seed a different label\",\"hi\":\"To give every seed a different label\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"To prevent herself from keeping notes\",\"hi\":\"To prevent herself from keeping notes\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"a69c2434-d627-4fd1-8a60-01458f18464e\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 3: The Ferry Painter\\n\\nEvery morning, Dev crossed the river by ferry and sketched the changing light on the water. One foggy day, the ferry was delayed, and impatient passengers complained. Dev used the extra time to draw their blurred reflections in the cabin windows. Weeks later, he displayed the sketch at a local exhibition. Several passengers recognised themselves and laughed at how serious they had looked. What had felt like wasted time became the subject of his most popular picture.\\n\\nQuestion: What did Dev draw during the delay?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 3: The Ferry Painter\\n\\nEvery morning, Dev crossed the river by ferry and sketched the changing light on the water. One foggy day, the ferry was delayed, and impatient passengers complained. Dev used the extra time to draw their blurred reflections in the cabin windows. Weeks later, he displayed the sketch at a local exhibition. Several passengers recognised themselves and laughed at how serious they had looked. What had felt like wasted time became the subject of his most popular picture.\\n\\nQuestion: What did Dev draw during the delay?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"A new ferry engine\",\"hi\":\"A new ferry engine\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Passengers’ blurred reflections\",\"hi\":\"Passengers’ blurred reflections\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"The exhibition building\",\"hi\":\"The exhibition building\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"A map of the river\",\"hi\":\"A map of the river\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"a4698be0-b258-42f2-b5d6-eb2600528426\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 4\\n\\nReliable public transport gives commuters an alternative to private vehicles. Frequency matters because passengers are less likely to use a service that (1) ___ long waits. Accurate information is equally valuable: when delays occur, travellers can (2) ___ their plans. Clean stations and safe walking routes also shape the overall (3) ___. Investment should therefore consider the complete journey, not (4) ___ the time spent inside a bus or train. A network becomes attractive when its parts work (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (5).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 4\\n\\nReliable public transport gives commuters an alternative to private vehicles. Frequency matters because passengers are less likely to use a service that (1) ___ long waits. Accurate information is equally valuable: when delays occur, travellers can (2) ___ their plans. Clean stations and safe walking routes also shape the overall (3) ___. Investment should therefore consider the complete journey, not (4) ___ the time spent inside a bus or train. A network becomes attractive when its parts work (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (5).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"separately\",\"hi\":\"separately\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"together\",\"hi\":\"together\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"loosely\",\"hi\":\"loosely\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"rarely\",\"hi\":\"rarely\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"a3ebfa47-8368-48c1-a4fd-7386b37980fd\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 2\\n\\nCities can reduce water waste by paying attention to small leaks. A dripping tap may appear (1) ___, yet many such leaks together can waste a large quantity of water. Regular inspection allows faults to be repaired (2) ___ they become expensive. Clear bills can also help households (3) ___ unusual changes in consumption. However, information alone is not (4) ___; people need simple ways to report problems. Conservation works best when authorities and residents act (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (4).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 2\\n\\nCities can reduce water waste by paying attention to small leaks. A dripping tap may appear (1) ___, yet many such leaks together can waste a large quantity of water. Regular inspection allows faults to be repaired (2) ___ they become expensive. Clear bills can also help households (3) ___ unusual changes in consumption. However, information alone is not (4) ___; people need simple ways to report problems. Conservation works best when authorities and residents act (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (4).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"sufficient\",\"hi\":\"sufficient\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"irrelevant\",\"hi\":\"irrelevant\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"harmful\",\"hi\":\"harmful\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"unnecessary\",\"hi\":\"unnecessary\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"a34ced26-8c8b-487e-8211-e3ca65d8de9d\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 2: The Value of Urban Wetlands\\n\\nAn urban wetland may look like unused land, especially during a dry season. Yet it can store rainwater, support birds and reduce pressure on drainage systems. Building over such an area may create immediate economic value, but it can also transfer hidden costs to nearby neighbourhoods through greater flood risk. Protecting a wetland does not mean preventing all public access. Paths and viewing areas can be designed carefully, provided that the most sensitive zones remain undisturbed.\\n\\nQuestion: Which title best suits the passage?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 2: The Value of Urban Wetlands\\n\\nAn urban wetland may look like unused land, especially during a dry season. Yet it can store rainwater, support birds and reduce pressure on drainage systems. Building over such an area may create immediate economic value, but it can also transfer hidden costs to nearby neighbourhoods through greater flood risk. Protecting a wetland does not mean preventing all public access. Paths and viewing areas can be designed carefully, provided that the most sensitive zones remain undisturbed.\\n\\nQuestion: Which title best suits the passage?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Why Every Wetland Should Become a Market\",\"hi\":\"Why Every Wetland Should Become a Market\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Birds That Avoid City Drainage\",\"hi\":\"Birds That Avoid City Drainage\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Dry Seasons and Public Ticket Prices\",\"hi\":\"Dry Seasons and Public Ticket Prices\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Urban Wetlands: Benefits Beyond Empty Space\",\"hi\":\"Urban Wetlands: Benefits Beyond Empty Space\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Comprehension Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"9eabc36f-3489-42c1-9cc7-0e5102a00587\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: What is the stated goal?\",\"hi\":\"Original Editorial-Style Passage 4: The Right to Repair\\n\\nWhen a small component fails, consumers are often told to replace an entire device. This may be convenient for manufacturers, but it increases costs and material waste. A stronger repair ecosystem would provide spare parts, diagnostic information and products designed to be opened without damage. Safety and intellectual property concerns are real, yet they should be addressed through proportionate rules rather than used to block every repair. The goal is not to force people to repair indefinitely; it is to make repair a practical option when it is safe and economical.\\n\\nQuestion: What is the stated goal?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"To require endless repair of every product\",\"hi\":\"To require endless repair of every product\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"To abolish product replacement completely\",\"hi\":\"To abolish product replacement completely\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"To transfer all repair work to consumers\",\"hi\":\"To transfer all repair work to consumers\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"To make safe and economical repair a practical choice\",\"hi\":\"To make safe and economical repair a practical choice\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"9cbc3675-c54d-4f6a-a0d0-88217b8cccac\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 3\\n\\nOnline training is useful only when participants can apply what they learn. Short demonstrations should be followed by tasks that (1) ___ the same skill. Immediate practice strengthens memory and reveals (2) ___ the instructions were clear. Trainers can then answer questions before confusion becomes (3) ___. A well-designed course also gives learners some (4) ___ over pace, while maintaining firm deadlines. This combination keeps the programme flexible (5) ___ focused.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (2).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 3\\n\\nOnline training is useful only when participants can apply what they learn. Short demonstrations should be followed by tasks that (1) ___ the same skill. Immediate practice strengthens memory and reveals (2) ___ the instructions were clear. Trainers can then answer questions before confusion becomes (3) ___. A well-designed course also gives learners some (4) ___ over pace, while maintaining firm deadlines. This combination keeps the programme flexible (5) ___ focused.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (2).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"whether\",\"hi\":\"whether\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"because\",\"hi\":\"because\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"unless\",\"hi\":\"unless\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"despite\",\"hi\":\"despite\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"9ac753af-76b1-4366-9a9e-204e4fb250dd\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 2: A Library on Wheels\\n\\nA district library converted an old bus into a mobile reading room. Every Tuesday, the bus visited three villages that had no permanent library. Its shelves carried children’s stories, exam guides and newspapers. Borrowers could return books during the next visit. After six months, the library noticed that teenagers were requesting more science books, so it changed part of the collection to match their interests.\\n\\nQuestion: Which quality did the library show by changing the collection?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 2: A Library on Wheels\\n\\nA district library converted an old bus into a mobile reading room. Every Tuesday, the bus visited three villages that had no permanent library. Its shelves carried children’s stories, exam guides and newspapers. Borrowers could return books during the next visit. After six months, the library noticed that teenagers were requesting more science books, so it changed part of the collection to match their interests.\\n\\nQuestion: Which quality did the library show by changing the collection?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Indifference\",\"hi\":\"Indifference\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Secrecy\",\"hi\":\"Secrecy\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Impatience\",\"hi\":\"Impatience\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Responsiveness\",\"hi\":\"Responsiveness\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"9ab5b86b-2f6b-47a2-96a1-cdaf78f70170\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 1: The Watchmaker’s Drawer\\n\\nLeena’s grandfather repaired watches in a narrow shop. One drawer was always locked, and Leena imagined that it held a rare gold watch. When her grandfather finally gave her the key, she found only broken springs, cracked glass and rusted gears. He explained that each piece came from a repair that had taught him something new. The drawer was valuable not because of what the pieces were worth, but because of the mistakes they helped him remember.\\n\\nQuestion: What was actually in the drawer?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 1: The Watchmaker’s Drawer\\n\\nLeena’s grandfather repaired watches in a narrow shop. One drawer was always locked, and Leena imagined that it held a rare gold watch. When her grandfather finally gave her the key, she found only broken springs, cracked glass and rusted gears. He explained that each piece came from a repair that had taught him something new. The drawer was valuable not because of what the pieces were worth, but because of the mistakes they helped him remember.\\n\\nQuestion: What was actually in the drawer?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Damaged watch parts\",\"hi\":\"Damaged watch parts\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Completed customer orders\",\"hi\":\"Completed customer orders\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Jewellery for sale\",\"hi\":\"Jewellery for sale\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"Family photographs\",\"hi\":\"Family photographs\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"994fd4e1-76f8-440d-88a6-5f64b337f79d\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 1: The Community Garden\\n\\nResidents of a crowded neighbourhood turned an unused plot into a community garden. Families volunteered on weekends, while a nearby school supplied compost made from its kitchen waste. The garden produced vegetables, but its greatest value was social. People who had rarely spoken began exchanging seeds, recipes and advice. The organisers later added a shaded bench so that older residents could participate without doing heavy work.\\n\\nQuestion: In the passage, “plot” most nearly means:\",\"hi\":\"Passage 1: The Community Garden\\n\\nResidents of a crowded neighbourhood turned an unused plot into a community garden. Families volunteered on weekends, while a nearby school supplied compost made from its kitchen waste. The garden produced vegetables, but its greatest value was social. People who had rarely spoken began exchanging seeds, recipes and advice. The organisers later added a shaded bench so that older residents could participate without doing heavy work.\\n\\nQuestion: In the passage, “plot” most nearly means:\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"the main events of a story\",\"hi\":\"the main events of a story\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"a secret plan\",\"hi\":\"a secret plan\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"a small piece of land\",\"hi\":\"a small piece of land\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"a chart of numbers\",\"hi\":\"a chart of numbers\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Basic Comprehension\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"98281d43-d000-42a7-8c56-b1979a2cf8c2\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 1\\n\\nProgress in any skill rarely comes from one dramatic effort. It usually depends on (1) ___ practice, honest feedback and enough patience to continue after mistakes. A learner who records errors can (2) ___ patterns that would otherwise remain hidden. This makes each new attempt more (3) ___. Rest is equally important because attention declines when the mind is (4) ___. A balanced routine is therefore more sustainable (5) ___ a schedule based only on pressure.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (1).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 1\\n\\nProgress in any skill rarely comes from one dramatic effort. It usually depends on (1) ___ practice, honest feedback and enough patience to continue after mistakes. A learner who records errors can (2) ___ patterns that would otherwise remain hidden. This makes each new attempt more (3) ___. Rest is equally important because attention declines when the mind is (4) ___. A balanced routine is therefore more sustainable (5) ___ a schedule based only on pressure.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (1).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"irregular\",\"hi\":\"irregular\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"consistent\",\"hi\":\"consistent\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"careless\",\"hi\":\"careless\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"brief\",\"hi\":\"brief\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"96a96ee3-2cd7-4e7a-bd36-891bcd99f38b\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Passage 3: Decision Fatigue\\n\\nPeople often assume that more choice always improves a decision. In practice, a long series of minor choices can consume attention needed for an important one. This does not mean that choice is undesirable. It suggests that routine decisions should be simplified where possible. A prepared checklist, a fixed time for recurring tasks or a clear default can reduce mental effort without removing meaningful control. The best simplification preserves the decisions that genuinely require thought.\\n\\nQuestion: What misconception does the passage challenge?\",\"hi\":\"Passage 3: Decision Fatigue\\n\\nPeople often assume that more choice always improves a decision. In practice, a long series of minor choices can consume attention needed for an important one. This does not mean that choice is undesirable. It suggests that routine decisions should be simplified where possible. A prepared checklist, a fixed time for recurring tasks or a clear default can reduce mental effort without removing meaningful control. The best simplification preserves the decisions that genuinely require thought.\\n\\nQuestion: What misconception does the passage challenge?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"That checklists can simplify routine work\",\"hi\":\"That checklists can simplify routine work\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"That important decisions require attention\",\"hi\":\"That important decisions require attention\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"That recurring tasks can have fixed times\",\"hi\":\"That recurring tasks can have fixed times\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"That more choice always leads to a better decision\",\"hi\":\"That more choice always leads to a better decision\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Comprehension Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"95b7d07c-8e88-4e70-b917-def7cd7bc550\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 1: Urban Heat Planning\\n\\nA city review of summer preparedness found that heat risk was not evenly distributed. Neighbourhoods with little shade and large paved areas stayed warmer for longer, while outdoor workers faced repeated exposure during peak afternoon hours. The review recommended cool-roof programmes, shaded bus stops, drinking-water points and work schedules that avoid the hottest period. It also warned that planting trees alone would not provide immediate relief because young trees take time to create shade. The report therefore favoured a mix of short-term protection and long-term design.\\n\\nQuestion: Why did the report not rely only on tree planting?\",\"hi\":\"Original Report-Style Passage 1: Urban Heat Planning\\n\\nA city review of summer preparedness found that heat risk was not evenly distributed. Neighbourhoods with little shade and large paved areas stayed warmer for longer, while outdoor workers faced repeated exposure during peak afternoon hours. The review recommended cool-roof programmes, shaded bus stops, drinking-water points and work schedules that avoid the hottest period. It also warned that planting trees alone would not provide immediate relief because young trees take time to create shade. The report therefore favoured a mix of short-term protection and long-term design.\\n\\nQuestion: Why did the report not rely only on tree planting?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"Trees always increase urban temperature.\",\"hi\":\"Trees always increase urban temperature.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"Residents refused every planting proposal.\",\"hi\":\"Residents refused every planting proposal.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"Trees cannot survive in any city.\",\"hi\":\"Trees cannot survive in any city.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"New trees take time to provide substantial shade.\",\"hi\":\"New trees take time to provide substantial shade.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"basic\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Current-Affairs Passages Based on a Report or Editorial\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"95772e75-9e40-40da-b062-cc41a138fb12\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Cloze Passage 2\\n\\nCities can reduce water waste by paying attention to small leaks. A dripping tap may appear (1) ___, yet many such leaks together can waste a large quantity of water. Regular inspection allows faults to be repaired (2) ___ they become expensive. Clear bills can also help households (3) ___ unusual changes in consumption. However, information alone is not (4) ___; people need simple ways to report problems. Conservation works best when authorities and residents act (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (3).\",\"hi\":\"Cloze Passage 2\\n\\nCities can reduce water waste by paying attention to small leaks. A dripping tap may appear (1) ___, yet many such leaks together can waste a large quantity of water. Regular inspection allows faults to be repaired (2) ___ they become expensive. Clear bills can also help households (3) ___ unusual changes in consumption. However, information alone is not (4) ___; people need simple ways to report problems. Conservation works best when authorities and residents act (5) ___.\\n\\nQuestion: Select the best word for blank (3).\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"erase\",\"hi\":\"erase\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"divide\",\"hi\":\"divide\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"forbid\",\"hi\":\"forbid\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"notice\",\"hi\":\"notice\"}},\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Cloze Passage\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0},{\"id\":\"952228fd-0be8-41de-b93f-9a970c88afc9\",\"question_text\":{\"en\":\"Original Story 4: The Envelope of Seeds\\n\\nNora received an unlabelled envelope of seeds from her aunt. Instead of planting all of them in one place, she divided them among four pots and changed only one condition in each: sunlight, water, soil or temperature. She kept notes for three weeks. Two pots produced similar shoots, one grew slowly and one did not sprout. Nora could not identify the plant yet, but she had learned which conditions helped the seeds begin to grow.\\n\\nQuestion: What does the story suggest about useful investigation?\",\"hi\":\"Original Story 4: The Envelope of Seeds\\n\\nNora received an unlabelled envelope of seeds from her aunt. Instead of planting all of them in one place, she divided them among four pots and changed only one condition in each: sunlight, water, soil or temperature. She kept notes for three weeks. Two pots produced similar shoots, one grew slowly and one did not sprout. Nora could not identify the plant yet, but she had learned which conditions helped the seeds begin to grow.\\n\\nQuestion: What does the story suggest about useful investigation?\"},\"options\":{\"A\":{\"en\":\"It must identify everything immediately.\",\"hi\":\"It must identify everything immediately.\"},\"B\":{\"en\":\"It should avoid written records.\",\"hi\":\"It should avoid written records.\"},\"C\":{\"en\":\"It can answer one question even when another remains open.\",\"hi\":\"It can answer one question even when another remains open.\"},\"D\":{\"en\":\"It succeeds only when every sample grows.\",\"hi\":\"It succeeds only when every sample grows.\"}},\"difficulty\":\"advanced\",\"source\":\"QuestionWale Original | Verified: SSC CGL English — Simple Passage Based on a Book or Story\",\"source_metadata\":null,\"year\":null,\"pyq_exam_name\":null,\"exam_tags\":[\"SSC\"],\"attempt_count\":0,\"correct_count\":0}],\"nextCursor\":\"952228fd-0be8-41de-b93f-9a970c88afc9\",\"hasMore\":true}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":300,"tags":["question-batch","question-batch:topic:v9:279c62cf-2025-456c-bf74-fcc9bacbd7e6"]}